# Database BACKUPS. These were not covered by the rules above — which match
# `production.sqlite` exactly — so every `production.sqlite.backup.<stamp>`
# file deploy.sh drops next to the live database was committable, and twelve
# of them were in fact committed and are still in this repository's history.
#
# They are not "just an old database". PrismaSessionStorage stores
# Session.accessToken in PLAINTEXT (only the app's own copies of tokens, in
# MigrationJob/SourceStore/etc., go through encryption.server.js), so each of
# these files is a list of live Shopify Admin API offline tokens for real
# merchant stores — several of which are byte-identical to the tokens in the
# live database right now. Broad glob, deliberately: the next backup will be
# named something nobody predicted.
prisma/*.sqlite
prisma/*.sqlite-shm
prisma/*.sqlite-wal
prisma/*.sqlite.backup*
prisma/*.backup.*
*.sqlite.backup*
.claude/settings.local.json

# SQLite sidecar files for the local dev database (WAL mode is enabled in
# db.server.js, so these two always exist alongside it).
/prisma/dev.sqlite-shm
/prisma/dev.sqlite-wal

# Guard against a nested dev database reappearing. Prisma resolves relative
# sqlite paths from the SCHEMA directory (prisma/), not the project root, so a
# DATABASE_URL of "file:./prisma/dev.sqlite" silently lands at
# prisma/prisma/dev.sqlite. That happened, and the resulting database held real
# OAuth tokens while being ignored by nothing. DATABASE_URL now reads
# "file:./dev.sqlite"; this rule stays so a regression cannot commit tokens.
prisma/prisma/
